![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

While you've fixed the invalid JSX, the `AuthButton` component example now has a reference error because the `email` and `password` variables are used without being defined. This makes the example incorrect and potentially confusing for an AI agent trying to use it. 

To make the example self-contained and valid, you should clarify where these values come from. The suggested code below defines them as constants with placeholder values and adds a comment to explain that in a real application, they would typically come from form state.

```suggestion
function AuthButton() {
  const { data: session, isPending } = useSession();
  if (isPending) return <div>Loading...</div>;

  if (session) {
    return (
      <div>
        <span>{session.user.email}</span>
        <button onClick={() => signOut()}>Sign Out</button>
      </div>
    );
  }

  // In a real app, email and password would come from form inputs.
  const email = "user@example.com";
  const password = "password123";

  return <button onClick={() => signIn.email({ email, password })}>Sign In</button>;
}
```
